            "text": "{\"study_topic_id\":\"study-de50ee503b22db74e606\",\"overview\":{\"text\":\"Six is a positive integer with notable roles in number theory and geometry. In number theory, it is the smallest perfect number: its positive divisors other than itself—1, 2, and 3—sum to 6. “Perfect” here has a precise arithmetic meaning, not a judgment about the number. In geometry, six is the side count of a hexagon, whose interior angles total 720°; in a regular hexagon, each of the six equal interior angles measures 120°. These examples show how six can describe both a special divisor pattern and a familiar polygon.\",\"source_urls\":[\"https://web.stanford.edu/class/archive/cs/cs106b/cs106b.1258/assignments/1-cpp/perfect\",\"https://www.math.cmu.edu/~bkell/21110-2010s/numbers.html\",\"https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/10-5-tessellations\"]},\"key_facts\":[{\"text\":\"A positive integer is perfect when its proper divisors—positive divisors other than itself—sum to the number.\",\"source_urls\":[\"https://web.stanford.edu/class/archive/cs/cs106b/cs106b.1258/assignments/1-cpp/perfect\"]},{\"text\":\"Six is the smallest perfect number: its proper divisors are 1, 2, and 3, and 1 + 2 + 3 = 6.\",\"source_urls\":[\"https://web.stanford.edu/class/archive/cs/cs106b/cs106b.1258/assignments/1-cpp/perfect\",\"https://www.math.cmu.edu/~bkell/21110-2010s/numbers.html\"]},{\"text\":\"A hexagon has six sides, and its interior angles total 720°.\",\"source_urls\":[\"https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/10-5-tessellations\"]},{\"text\":\"Each interior angle of a regular hexagon is 120°; three such angles meet at a point in a regular-hexagon tessellation.\",\"source_urls\":[\"https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/10-5-tessellations\"]}],\"sources\":[{\"title\":\"CS106B Perfect numbers\",\"url\":\"https://web.stanford.edu/class/archive/cs/cs106b/cs106b.1258/assignments/1-cpp/perfect\",\"publisher\":\"Stanford University\",\"retrieved_at\":\"2026-09-24T18:42:45.901460+00:00\",\"supports\":\"Defines perfect numbers and proper divisors; states that 6 is the first perfect number and verifies it with 1 + 2 + 3 = 6.\"},{\"title\":\"21-110: Additional topics in number theory\",\"url\":\"https://www.math.cmu.edu/~bkell/21110-2010s/numbers.html\",\"publisher\":\"Carnegie Mellon University\",\"retrieved_at\":\"2026-09-24T18:42:45.901460+00:00\",\"supports\":\"Defines proper divisors and perfect numbers, and confirms that 6 is the smallest perfect number with proper divisors 1, 2, and 3.\"},{\"title\":\"10.5 Tessellations - Contemporary Mathematics\",\"url\":\"https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/10-5-tessellations\",\"publisher\":\"OpenStax, Rice University\",\"retrieved_at\":\"2026-09-24T18:42:45.901460+00:00\",\"supports\":\"States that regular hexagon interior angles are 120°, describes three hexagons meeting at a vertex, and illustrates hexagons formed from six equilateral triangles.\"}],\"research_queries\":[\"site:math.wisc.edu perfect numbers 6 proper divisors\",\"site:edu \\\"perfect number\\\" \\\"6\\\" divisors mathematics\",\"site:openstax.org hexagon interior angles 720\",\"site:mathworld.wolfram.com perfect number six sum divisors definition\",\"site:mathsisfun.com hexagon six sides interior angle sum\",\"site:britannica.com six number mathematics perfect number hexagon\"],\"notes\":\"Overview is 90 words. Claims about perfect numbers are supported by the opened Stanford and Carnegie Mellon pages; the hexagon angle claims are supported by the opened OpenStax text. The description’s quiz material was treated only as context.\",\"unresolved_concerns\":[],\"self_check_complete\":true}"
